A CSP has the variables P<sub>1</sub>, P<sub>2</sub>, P<sub>3</sub>, P<sub>4</sub> with the domains D<sub>1</sub> = {1, 4, 5}, D<sub>2</sub> = {2, 3}, D<sub>3</sub> = {5, 6} and D<sub>4</sub> = {1, 6}, and two global constraints:

- Atmost(12, P<sub>1</sub>, P<sub>2</sub>, P<sub>3</sub>, P<sub>4</sub>): the sum of the four values is at most 12.
- Alldiff(P<sub>1</sub>, P<sub>2</sub>, P<sub>3</sub>, P<sub>4</sub>).

Propagate the constraints as described in the book (Section 5.2.5). For Atmost, delete the maximum value of a domain whenever it is inconsistent with the minimum values of the other domains. For Alldiff, delete the value of every singleton domain from the other domains. Repeat until no domain changes. What is the result?

**Select one alternative:**

- A. D<sub>1</sub> = {1, 4}, D<sub>2</sub> = {2, 3}, D<sub>3</sub> = {5, 6}, D<sub>4</sub> = {1}
- B. D<sub>1</sub> = {4}, D<sub>2</sub> = {2}, D<sub>3</sub> = {5}, D<sub>4</sub> = {1}
- C. D<sub>1</sub> = {4}, D<sub>2</sub> = {2, 3}, D<sub>3</sub> = {5, 6}, D<sub>4</sub> = {1}
- D. An inconsistency is detected at once, since the sum of the maximum values, 5 + 3 + 6 + 6 = 20, exceeds 12.
- E. An inconsistency is detected, since 12 − (1 + 2 + 5 + 1) = 3 is smaller than both values in D<sub>3</sub>, so D<sub>3</sub> becomes empty.
